11|0@martialarts^1438655426#ctqnwc4:Toptomcat$:&gt;During the time of Kano sensei, Japan was modernizing rapidly and emerging as a world power. As society put more emphasis on education and meritocracy, Kano sensei also see the decline of martial arts in modern Japan, as new technologies and idea flooded into the island. Because of this, he believes that Judo should not emphasize on destroying your opponent like many of the old-fashion jujitsu school during his time. Instead, being a college graduate, sensei Kano see the importance of physical education and promotion of peace (he's not a big fan of the military and their expansion in Asia). These values should be the forefront and doctrine of Judo in his words. Further distancing Judo away from the more combat-orientated jujitsu school of the time. It's true that Kano emphasized physical education and a pacifistic ethic, making a deliberate attempt to break from the more mean-spirited and disreputable elements of jujutsu. But he also strongly emphasized cross-training with sumo, catch wrestling, and other grappling arts. And he sent judoka to mixed-rules competitions with catch wrestlers, jujutsuka, and even boxers and savateurs to prove the art's practical effectiveness in unarmed combat and stimulate interest in judo. The judo of Kano's time *was* a mixed martial art: any definition of the 'spirit of judo' that excludes modern MMA would have to be pretty convoluted if it wasn't also to exclude the Kodokan judo of 1880-1938.
2|0@martialarts^1439750566#cu52kf2:mattBernius$:It's also worth noting that Judo was a direct result of the importation of English and European ideals/philosophy into Japan. Kano was heavily influenced by the Muscular Christian (England) and Gymnastics (Continental European) movements. This led directly to much of the ethos of Judo. Its also why Kano was a huge supporter of the Olympic movement which grew out of Muscular Christianity and Gymnastics. In that respect, it was arguably one of the first truly international martial arts (though Ju Jitsu had paved the way in Europe during the mid ninetieth century).
