1|0@martialarts^1438716421#ctrfssy:osotogary_$:I love tai otoshi, I feel it really fits my build. I find that uchimata is a great set up for kouchi gari. I also like a good osoto gari if you time it right. I do alright with ken ken uchimata and seio otoshi. I'm too tall for seionage. I've also been working on my shot since I joined a more MMA oriented sambo gym. It's still slow as shit, but I've caught a few people with a single. I love judo, but I got frustrated with the rule changes that effect me as an average guy, but are meant to keep judo relevant in the Olympics, and keep revenue coming into the IJF. The gym I'm at now competes in all different venues. My first ever competition was a few weeks ago, at a Mongolian wrestling competition. I'm lined up to compete in Shuai Jiao in September. Maybe I'll go back to judo, but definitely not for a while if the IJF keeps doing stupid shit and putting their revenues before the welfare of the athletes.
0|0@martialarts^1438723680#ctrkv6p:Blackbelt_joe$:Yeah I feel you. I love Judo too. When they did those silly rule change, it also crippled me greatly since one of my bread and butter is Kata Guruma transition into kouchigari. I really love the etiquette and spirit of Ippon. That completing in a 'right way' is just as important as winning. If one wins but won it in a way with shortcuts... that's no honor. That's not IPPON. It's integrity. Just like the difference between Martin Luther King Jr. and Malcolm X, both great civil rights icon. But while MLK fight for civil rights with non violence doctrine, the latter promote violent behavior towards whites. They are both fighting for a noble cause and both comes from a sense of justice, but Dr. King definitely demonstrate integrity at the face of tyranny. Integrity and honor aren't just some marketing tools in Judo, you actually have to live it.
2|0@martialarts^1438725076#ctrlrxh:osotogary_$:It's throwing someone on their back, dude. It's not really all that different from a tapout or a tko. It is the way it is because it's symbolic of defeat. It's also certainly not comparable to the American civil rights movement. I think you're demonstrating a lot of ignorance towards Malcolm X, MMA and the history of Judo itself. I suggest that you read The Way of Judo: A Portrait of Jigoro Kano and His Students by John Stevens. It discusses a lot of Kano's philosophy towards judo, the intertwined histories of judo and English catch as catch can wrestling, and the fighting careers of many of Kano's greatest students, which I believe mirror Rousey's career in many ways. I also think that if you're really interested, you should do some research on Anderson Silva. To me he represents many of the virtues that the traditional martial arts value, and fights with what you'd call the "right way", despite having a style that emphasizes stand up striking.
