14|0@martialarts^1438649246#ctqkb25:vertical_punch$:To be honest, I think the exact opposite of you. If judo were really all about physical education, they would embrace BJJ, wrestling, and sambo influences. Instead, they appear to ban legitimate kano-syllabus techniques when competitors from other combats sports use them *too* effectively. If judo wasn't about money, they wouldn't care about Olympic TV ratings and would let newaza go longer than one minute. They also wouldnt care if it "looked too much like wrestling" and would allow leg attacks. Now don't get me wrong, it is really annoying when people spam single leg attempts all day and never learn to throw. It is also super annoying when people stall on the mats. I do feel that you have to incentivize the skills you want to develop, so there must be some sort of trade off. I dont know what it should be either, not like I am so smart. But if it were up to me, I would make multiple events. Like there would be a no-gi event, events for both limited and unlimited newaza, events for leg attacks and no leg attacks. You would get specialists sure, but you would also get a lot of cross training and that would raise the level of everything. As it stands now, if you want the complete Kano syllabus, you have to cross train in BJJ, sambo, and greco which is probably not what Kano was going for. 
-6|0@martialarts^1438653395#ctqmqef:Blackbelt_joe$:Well, if you do some research on Judo in Japan. Maybe you will have a different opinion. Judo in Japan is always focus on IPPON. Even, when one can win with just points alone by stalling, Japanese Judoka will always go for IPPON even when they are winning with points. Because that is the "right way" to win in Japan and in the spirit of Kano and JUdo. European and others on the other hand really emphasize on winning, hence u see all the stalling and such when one is already ahead by points. That is something that IJF really want to change but when different culture are learning your sport, u can't expect them to adopt EVERYTHING that you taught. Hence it also express the difference of culture where in Japan, one can't only just win but need to win in certain way, "the right way", as they see it... with IPPON... in contrast westerner usually just focus on the winning part and integrity second. 
6|0@martialarts^1438657609#ctqp444:nanjingbooj$:I studied Judo in Japan starting in Japanese highschool when I was a student there. That was quite awhile ago. Probably longer ago than the age of most members in this subreddit. I think most people abroad don't understand the spirit in which judo was created and is still practiced in Japan. They don't understand that judo is compulsory for a year in all highschools even. That it was/is developed as a method to strengthen the nation spiritually and athletically without a military mindset. That the average professional judoka is so completely different than the average MMA fighter in mindset, it is unreal. That judo was developed in order NOT to hurt their uke in training as well as competition. And, in that understanding, any throws or locks that were inherently unsafe for the uke were removed by Kano Jigoro sensei. Ippon is the ultimate ideal of judo in Japan. Its not about gaming the system (for most people) in Japan. It does not include strikes because Jigoro made that choice. Not because Tenjin Shinyo-ryu had no strikes. In fact the whole system is pretty much predicated on the idea that strikes can soften up an uke or misbalance them in order to make a throw or lock easier. But Jigoro rejected the idea for his sport (but kept many striking kata because he believed it was important for the art, not sport). IMO Judo can dovetail nicely with what is happening in MMA, but to say the former is somehow reliant on the later for survival of the sport is downright silly. 
-4|0@martialarts^1438707062#ctr98mf:Blackbelt_joe$:looks like you're the only one who understand what I'm getting at. Judo isn't some self-defense system. It's a way of life. It's the teaching of Kano and it's emphasis on scientific approach that makes it different. It teaches integrity because one can't only win but has to win with IPPON. Always go for the IPPON, just like Yamashita and Inoue back in their prime. Inoue can easily win out by points at the Olympic qualifier... but it's either IPPON or NOTHING. He lost that match, but he won as a Judoka. That's something all these knuckleheads in this sub won't understand. Because all them care about is who is winning and what is the most effective. Go get a Gun if you're so into winning a fight. 
8|0@martialarts^1438649640#ctqkj98:BlackdogLao$:Then Judo will unfortunately die a death, in the same way that Jigoro Kano went against the grain of the old-fashioned jujitsu of his time, because he sensed the Zeitgeist of a rapidly changing Japan needed something beside militant martial arts that emphasised destruction or death of an opponent, Now Judo is the old fashion giant that, if it doesn't change will be toppled by the new kid on the block, MMA, which has expertly tapped into today's Zeitgeist, and delivered a much needed dose of reality into a industry that paradoxically took itself far too serious with the mystique and awe surrounding the coveted "black belt" while at the same time suffering from a complete lack of professionalism, a lack of oversight/quality control, and no recognisable structure of rank in line with modern educational standards. (how many black belts equals a PhD?) Judo doesn't have to change, but like all those old fashioned Ju-jitsu schools of Japan in the early 1900's, they would be foolish not to move with the times.
