5|0@martialarts^1438691089#ctr09u8:BlackdogLao$:I'm not actually "talking a bunch of trash", when I referred to the industry, I was talking about the martial arts as a whole, not Judo specifically, I have a huge respect for Judo, I have cross trained it myself and have been complimented on my signature (no gi version) Tai Otoshi. I've also coached a couple local Judoka in MMA and they have had great success in the transition. My local Judo clubs regularly are invited to coach seminars at my club, and both clubs have benefited from the relationship. I am in no way a critic of Judo. However, no matter how respectable the art of Judo, nor how capable the average practitioner was, many martial arts clubs (almost always other styles other than judo) have incredible low standards, unfit practitioners filled with empty empowerment that would most likely get them killed in an actual fight, that they might well have been able to avoid or walk away from if they hadn't believed themselves to be so invincible due to ego massaging yet ultimately useless training. In spite of the usually consistent and good quality standards at most judo clubs, the martial arts industry at a whole was at an all time low standards wise, until MMA came along, and largely cleaned house, in it's first incarnation, MMA pitted styles against each other to finally dispel the myths and awe that surrounded styles that had grown lazy and ineffective. Nowadays MMA, rather than pitting styles against one another, showcases much more well rounded fighters, who MUST be versed in some style or art that promotes effective standing striking, standing takedowns and takedown defence, and submission and positional knowledge on the ground. if a fighter is missing knowledge in one of these areas, their weakness is quickly exposed. the separate styles that fighters practice in order to gain skills in one or more of these area are a useful short-list of effective styles that have been pressure tested by a reality based, peer review method and have come out of it with merit or distinction. Judo could benefit from a strong relationship with MMA as MMA is the shortcut way for the average person looking to start a martial art to make an informed choice about which stlye to practice. making an informed choice just based on the sales pitch of say Wing Chun, Aikido and Judo could be very difficult for an untrained person, but by actually seeing those styles prove their worth in a realistic combat sports setting, allows a style that has actual use against a resisting opponent to shine. This means that if Judo embraces MMA, it could see an increase in people taking up Judo, not just to use their judo in MMA, lets face it, not everyone who takes up a sport want to compete at a high level, these people may just want to find a local club to train at for the health benefits, for self defence, and to meet new, like minded people. Judo has more to gain by understanding the benefits of a better relationship with MMA.
-6|0@martialarts^1438706102#ctr8koy:Blackbelt_joe$:Nah, Judo doesn't need MMA. MMA needs Judo. 
2|0@martialarts^1439602828#cu3h73d:HKBFG$:i would argue both are correct
